    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class (with `AcceptanceBase` being abstract), and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `capacityWarning` flag so that a newly created owner is flagged `true` when its city already holds between 40 and 49 owners (approaching the capacity limit of 50), while leaving the hard 409 rejection at 50 unchanged.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean property to the `OwnerDto` schema (the DTO is generated from this spec at build time, and MapStruct auto-maps it from the model by name).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (model) \u2014 Added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with its getter/setter, mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added a `CITY_CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40` constant and, in `addOwner`, computed the city's owner count once (reusing it for the existing hard-limit check) and set `owner.setCapacityWarning(ownersInCity >= 40)`. Since the create is already rejected when the count is \u2265 50, this flags exactly the 40\u201349 range.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 Added the `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` column. The test profile uses `hsqldb` (with `ddl-auto=none`), so the column is required there; the others were updated for consistency.",
